<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.3.2: Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01606</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.3.2: Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division</md:title>
    <md:uuid>20a6bcb2-b348-4e2c-a9b6-c32b34f02084</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces students to using the process of long division to divide polynomials. In the same way that numbers are divided using long division, polynomials can also be divided. While the process is similar, students will learn to use placeholders, such as \(0x^2\), to stand in for a missing term of each degree in a polynomial dividend.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Display the traditional division problem \(960 \div 20\) to students. As a class, work through each individual step, displayed below. If more guidance is needed describing each step, see the Additional Resources section for the detailed description of a similar problem.</para>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,48\\20\left){\vphantom{1{960}}}\right.
\!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{960}}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - 80} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,160\\\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - 160} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]</para>
<para>Explain that we will use this process to divide polynomials. Work together to solve the division expression, \((m^2+9m+20) \div (m+4)\), provided in the activity. Ask students to describe each step in the process as they have just done it in the long division problem above. Emphasize that the divisor, \(20\), was one quantity in the process above, so \((m + 4)\) will be one quantity in the process here. Help guide each step if any direction is needed.</para>
<para>Allow time for students to complete the remaining exercises individually.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->MLR 2 Collect and Display: Conversing</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content--><para>As students describe the taditional division problem \(960 \div 20\) a class, listen for and collect the language students use to describe the long division process. Write the students' words and phrases on a visual display and update it throughout the remainder of the lesson. Remind students to borrow language from the display as needed. This will help students read and use mathematical language during their partner and whole-group discussions.
</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Design Principle(s): Maximize meta-awareness</para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title"><!--Extra Support Title-->Provide support for students</para>
<!-- Extra Support Content--><para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ff4abd7c9bec5ca1a1a7d070b61658237abfbde7">Distribute graphic organizers</link> to the students to assist them with participating in this routine. </para>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->Action and Expression: Organizational problem-solving skills</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content--><para>Provide students with graph paper or use lined paper turned sideways to help keep factors lined up within the worked solution.
</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Supports accessibility for Organizing, Visual-Spatial Processing </para>
  </div>
</div>

<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>Recall the process of dividing \(960 \div 20\) as a class using long division. Use this same method to divide the polynomials below. After each problem, check in with a partner. With your partner, discuss your work and use the words dividend, divisor, and quotient in your explanations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the quotient using long division.</item>
  <para>\((m^2+9m+20) \div (m+4)\) </para>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work or use the following video if you need help</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a9d49c5aee05915f41f403fa42e48dfb7088304d"/></media></div>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(m + 5\);</para>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}m + 4\mathop{\left){\vphantom{1{{m^2} + 9m + 20}}}\right.
  \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{m^2} + 9m + 20}}}}
  \limits^{\displaystyle\,\,\, {m\,\, + \,\,5}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({m^2} + 4m)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,5m + 20\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\, - (5m + 20)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the quotient using long division.</item>
  <para>\((p^2+2p-8) \div (p^2-7p+10)\)</para>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1+ \frac{9p-18}{p^2-7p+10}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the quotient using long division.</item>
  <para>\((5x+5) \div (x+5)\) </para>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(5- \frac{20}{x+5}\) </para>
<note>
  <para>Notice in the next problem, there is no \(x^3\) term in the dividend. Add \(0x^3\) as a placeholder when using long division.</para>
</note>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the quotient using long division.</item>
  <para>\((x^4-11x^2-7x-6) \div (x+3)\) </para>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x^3-3x^2-2x-1-\frac{3}{x+3}\) </para>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}x + 3\,\,\mathop{\left){\vphantom{1{{x^4} + 0{x^3} -11{x^2} -7x - 6}}}\right. \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{x^4} + 0{x^3} -11{x^2} -7x - 6}}}} \limits^{\displaystyle {x^3\,\, -3x^2 \,\,-2x\,\,-1}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - {x^4} + 3{x^3}} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,-3x^3 -11x^2 -7x -6\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, -3{x^3} -9{x^2}} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,-2x^2 -7x -6\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\, -2{x^2} -6x} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,-x-6\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\, -x -3} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,-3\end{array}\] </para>

<note>
  <para>In this next problem, you will need to add two placeholders to divide.</para>
</note>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the quotient using long division.</item>
  <para> \((125x^3-8) \div (5x-2)\)</para>
</list>
<para> Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(25x^2+10x+4\)</para>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}5x - 2\,\,\mathop{\left){\vphantom{1{125{x^3} + 0{x^2} +0x - 8}}}\right. \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{125{x^3} + 0{x^2} +0x - 8}}}} \limits^{\displaystyle {25^2\,\, +10x \,\,+4}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - 125{x^3} -50{x^2}} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,50x^2 +0x -8\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, -50{x^2} -20x} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,20x -8\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline {\, -20x -8} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\] </para>

<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title> Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Find the quotient using long division.</para>
<para>\((125y^5-80y^4-31y^3+37y^2+10y-24) \div (5y-4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(25y^4+4y^3-3y^2+5y+6\)</para>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}5y - 4\mathop{\left){\vphantom{1{125{y^5} - 80{y^4} - 31{y^3} + 37{y^2} + 10y - 24}}}\right.
  \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{125{y^5} - 80{y^4} - 31{y^3} + 37{y^2} + 10y - 24}}}}
  \limits^{\displaystyle\,\,\, {25{y^4} + \,\,\,4{y^3}\,\, - \,\,3{y^2}\,\, + \,\,5y\,\,\, + \,\,6\,}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (125{y^5} - 100{y^4})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,20{y^4} - 31{y^3}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (20{y^4} - 16{y^3})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, - \,15{y^3} + 37{y^2}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ( - \,15{y^3} + 12{y^2})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,25{y^2} + 10y\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (25{y^2} - 20y)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,30y - 24\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (30y - 24)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>As students complete the polynomial long division process, students may try to break apart a binomial divisor into individual monomials. Emphasize that the divisor will always be a single quantity in this process.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share questions from the activity. Address any misconceptions about the process. Use the following key concepts to help reinforce the process:</para>
<list>
  <item> Long division can be performed on polynomials using a very similar process to how it is done using number quantities. </item>
  <item> When performing long division on polynomials, as in other long division, making sure all values are properly aligned is helpful to streamline the process and avoid errors. </item>
  <item> When the dividend is missing terms of each degree from the highest degree down to a degree of zero, add in placeholders with a coefficient of \(0\). </item>
  <item> When you find a remainder, it should be written as a fraction with the remainder in the numerator and the divisor in the denominator. </item>
  <item> You can check your solution by multiplying it by the divisor. The product should be equal to the dividend in the original problem. </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to divide polynomials using long division. </para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/755cfca7054e90660bbf3c13db6d5d6cda8c6534"/></media></div>


<title>6.3.2: Self Check</title>
<!--BEGIN SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<!--SELF CHECK QUESTION GOES BEFORE THE Table -->
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Find the quotient: \((3x^3-19x^2-9x-35) \div (x-7)\).</para>
<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(3x^2+40x+5\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the second term in your long division. Did you make a mistake with a sign? The answer is \(3x^2+2x+5\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(3x^2+2x+5\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiply \((x-7)(3x^2+2x+5)\). The product is \(3x^3-19x^2-9x-35\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(3x^2+2x-23\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the third term in your long division. Did you make a mistake with a sign? The answer is \(3x^2+2x+5\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^2+2x+5\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the first term in your long division. The answer is \(3x^2+2x+5\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!--END SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->


<title>6.3.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division</title>
<para>To divide a polynomial by a binomial, we follow a procedure very similar to long division of numbers. So let’s look carefully at the steps we take when we divide a \(3\)-digit number, \(875\), by a \(2\)-digit number, \(25\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/68e3a4fec0f507563873765a4fe4ee171c2c30e9"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Starting from the leftmost digit in the dividend and moving right one digit at a time, find the smallest value possible that is larger than the divisor.</para>
<para><emphasis>Divide </emphasis>this value, \(87\), by the divisor, \(25\). Write the whole-number result with no remainder in the space for the quotient.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6d560526b876d277111b2e64beb88e7c1e8fe79b"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> <emphasis>Multiply</emphasis> this digit in the quotient by the divisor, \(3 \cdot 25\). </para>
<para>Write the result below the corresponding digits in the dividend.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2424aa7c0cb5bbe104d266fb3a46fb8378005464"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> <emphasis>Subtract</emphasis> the two values.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e79e0186961e84a73a14b91343ad3660411c6026"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -  Bring down</emphasis> the next digit in the dividend.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b9ca5b6670206d68848ee6faa18cde874d62a148"/></media></figure></para>
<para> <emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Repeat the division by dividing the new value, \(125\), by the divisor. </para>
<para>Write the result, \(5\), over the corresponding digit in the quotient.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4c18343a0a016fdf58a81555a6386d62131b51d7"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -  Multiply </emphasis>this digit in the quotient by the divisor, \(5 \cdot 25\). </para>
<para>Write the result at the bottom below the previous number.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d62338cb93a56ea6c55187ae25a16b905ce1b16e"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> <emphasis>Subtract </emphasis>the two values. Since there are no more digits to bring down, this value is the remainder. </para>
<para>The quotient is on top.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b7f640649ba205c40c24e872024a7b61362cb3dc"/></media></figure></para>
<para>We check division by multiplying the quotient by the divisor.</para>
<para>If we did the division correctly, the product should equal the dividend.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(35 \cdot 25\)</item>
  <item>\(875{\style{color:red}&amp;checkmark;}\)</item>
</list>
<para>Now we will divide a trinomial by a binomial. As you read through the Example, notice how similar the steps are to the numerical Example above.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the quotient: \((x^2+9x+20) \div (x+5)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Write it as a long division problem. Be sure the dividend is in standard form.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/04b21d17dec9baa3194d6cf86af5aa4cba8bfc6e"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Divide \(x^2\) by \(x\). It may help to ask yourself, “What do I need to multiply \(x\) by to get \(x^2\)?”</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25c052f7dfcf4f98a83014971ff933cac8225641"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Put the answer, \(x\), in the quotient over the \(x\) term. Multiply \(x\) times \(x + 5\). Line up the like terms under the dividend. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7dde891929abfcb23e3379c9ac1429fe2be8f912"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Subtract \(x^2 + 5x\) from \(x^2 + 9x\). You may find it easier to change the signs and then add. Then bring down the last term, \(20\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2ee31bc0482996e6b2d393e7a17fdc4f1059ac6e"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Divide \(4x\) by \(x\). It may help to ask yourself, “What do I need to multiply \(x\) by to get \(4x\)?” Put the answer, \(4\), in the quotient over the constant term. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b26317c879d6f7c2be57f39f39eaf7ea98cafd3b"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6  - </emphasis>Multiply \(4\) times \(x+5\). </para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9f846bcf44e244891a5ec74e633cec9f296cd35f"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Subtract \(4x+20\) from \(4x+20\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f502a39bb2e7f7c5bdd58309d2c0c45f6f5165c1"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 8 -</emphasis> Check: Multiply the quotient by the divisor. You should get the dividend.</para>
<para> \((x+4)(x+5)\)
  \(x^2+9x+20{\style{color:red}&amp;checkmark;}\)</para>
<para>When we divided \(875\) by \(25\), we had no remainder. But sometimes division of numbers does leave a remainder. The same is true when we divide polynomials. In the next Example, we’ll have division that leaves a remainder. We write the remainder as a fraction with the divisor as the denominator.</para>
<para>Look back at the dividend in the previous Example. The terms were written in descending order of degrees, and there were no missing degrees. The dividend in the next Example will be \(x^4-x^2+5^x-6\). It is missing an \(x^3\) term. We will add in \(0x^3\) as a placeholder.</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the quotient: \((x^4-x^2+5x-6) \div (x+2)\).</para>
<para>Notice that there is no \(x^3\) term in the dividend. We will add \(0x^3\) as a placeholder.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Write it as a long division problem. </para>
<para>Be sure the dividend is in standard form with placeholders for missing terms.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b4941ec2c446588b4596a562e5ea96998c9e7a15"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Divide \(x^4\) by \(x\).</para>
<para> Put the answer, \(x^3\), in the quotient over the \(x^3\) term. Multiply \(x^3\) times \(x+2\). </para>
<para>Line up the like terms. Subtract and then bring down the next term.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2c4804a6043e02f1311f02cfb091a6f239203837"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Divide \(-2x^3\) by \(x\).</para>
<para> Put the answer, \(-2x^2\), in the quotient over the \(x^2\) term. </para>
<para>Multiply \(-2x^2\) times \(x+1\). Line up the like terms. </para>
<para>Subtract and bring down the next term.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ed590d433a425ef0a479df42453d4532ed686fbd"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Divide \(3x^2\) by \(x\).</para>
<para> Put the answer, \(3x\), in the quotient over the \(x\) term. </para>
<para>Multiply \(3x\) times \(x+1\). </para>
<para>Line up the like terms. Subtract and bring down the next term.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/17fb04d990983a4072ae9fc64ecfdc293ce4f399"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Divide \(-x\) by \(x\). Put the answer, \(-1\), in the quotient over the constant term. </para>
<para>Multiply \(-1\) times \(x+1\). Line up the like terms. </para>
<para>Change the signs and then add. </para>
<para>Write the remainder as a fraction with the divisor as the denominator.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/95ecf79fb59faf56055af66b8da6194196f3c932"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis>To check, multiply \((x+2)(x^3−2x^2+3x−1−\frac{4}{x+2})\). </para>
<para>The result should be \(x^4−x^2+5x−6\).</para>
<para>In the next Example, we will divide by \(2a+3\). </para>
<para>As we divide, we will have to consider the coefficients as well as the variables.</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the quotient: \((8a^3+27) \div (2a+3)\).</para>
<para>This time, we will show the division all in one step. We need to add two placeholders to divide.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a82608c14e7967eca0a11aa4a99ea573bb767853"/></media></figure></para>
<para>To check, multiply \((2a+3)(4a^2-6a+9)\).</para>
<para>The result should be \(8a^3+27\).</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 4</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the quotient: \((2x+14) \div (x+7)\). Can you see a quick way to solve this?</para>
 <para>
    \begin{array}{r}
    2\phantom{)} \\
    x+7{\overline{\smash{\big)}\,2x+14}}\\
    \underline{-~\phantom{(}(2x+14)}\\
    0\\
    \end{array}
  </para>
<para>The answer is 2.</para>


<title>Try It: Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division</title>
<para>Find the quotients.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((y^2+10y+21) \div (y+3)\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((x^4-7x^2+7x+6) \div (x+3)
  \)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((x^3-64) \div (x-4)
  \)</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title> Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to divide these polynomials using long division:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
  y + 7\); </item>
</list>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}y + 3\mathop{\left){\vphantom{1{{y^2} + 10y + 21}}}\right.
\!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{y^2} + 10y + 21}}}}
\limits^{\displaystyle\,\,\, {y\,\, + \,\,7}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({y^2} + 7y)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,3y + 21\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (3y + 21)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^3-3x^2+2x+1+\frac{3}{x+3}\); </item>
</list>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,{x^3} - 3{x^2} + 2x + 1\\x + 3\sqrt {{x^4} + 0{x^3} - 7{x^2} + 7x + 6} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({x^4} + 3{x^3})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, - 3{x^3} - 7{x^2}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ( - 3{x^3} - 9{x^2})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,2{x^2} + 7x\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (2{x^2} + 6x)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,x + 6\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (x + 3)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,3\end{array}\]</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
<item>\(
    x^2+4x+16\); </item>
</list>
<para>\[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,{x^2} + 4x + 16\\x - 4\left){\vphantom{1{{x^3} + 0{x^2} + 0x - 64}}}\right.
\!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{x^3} + 0{x^2} + 0x - 64}}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({x^3} - 4{x^2})} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,4{x^2} + 0x\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (4{x^2} - 16x)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,16x - 64\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - (16x - 64)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]</para>
</section>
